### 1.2.5 Communication Standardization and Extensibility

The custom control circuit presented in this document was developed to convert a manually operated ice cream machine into an automatic mechanism. This design serves as a reference implementation for the specific ice cream machine and is not a mandatory hardware configuration for all devices integrated into the kiosk system.
The system is designed according to a standardized architecture in which Edge-controlled peripheral machines are integrated through an RS485 interface. Each Edge-controlled peripheral must provide a compatible RS485 interface, a documented control protocol, and a corresponding Edge plugin driver. Devices may use their own control circuits and actuators. RS485 defines the physical communication layer; it does not by itself define device commands, frame structure, status codes, checksum rules, or machine-specific behavior.
Using RS485 as the standard communication interface provides a consistent connection method between the Edge Computer and peripheral devices. The Edge Computer communicates with the RS485 bus through the USB-to-RS485 Converter, while controllers such as the STM32 use UART together with the TTL-to-RS485 Converter Module (MAX485). This design reduces differences between device types, simplifies the integration process, and supports system scalability.
Peripheral devices may have different electrical designs, controllers, and actuation mechanisms. However, each peripheral directly controlled by the Edge Computer must provide a compatible RS485 interface, a documented control protocol, and a corresponding Edge plugin driver. The Edge plugin driver is responsible for opening the serial connection, translating Edge operations into device-specific commands, validating responses, and handling communication errors. A common application protocol is not assumed unless it is explicitly specified by the system.
Therefore, the diagrams in this section describe a specific implementation for the ice cream machine while also providing a reference for integrating other peripheral devices into the kiosk system.